gm-method (closes the cpu-gpu fractal-parity arc): bit-exact CPU emulation of a GPU GLSL fractal is NOT portably achievable, proven by measured elimination - do not keep spawning fround variants (shotgun anti-pattern). Findings: (1) per-op Math.fround on the _bin/_u op chokepoint cuts the float64-vs-float32 gap ~2.5m -> ~0.7-2.3m and is the PORTABLE FLOOR; (2) frounding MORE (dot/length/normalize/mat3mul per-component) makes it WORSE because the GPU uses FMA (fused multiply-add = ONE rounding for a*b+c, not two) and wider dot-product accumulators - per-component fround over-rounds; (3) the GPU compiler (FXC/ANGLE) REORDERS the unrolled multi-octave fractal arbitrarily, and the rounding differs per GPU VENDOR - so any fround tuning that closes the gap on the witness GPU (e.g. SwiftShader) over-fits and mismatches the user's real GPU. THEREFORE exact CPU==GPU is only reachable by the CPU reading the GPU's ACTUAL output (a shared baked heightfield / a 1px probe readback), which requires a GPU where the consumer runs - a HEADLESS server (physics collider) has none, so it's architecturally blocked there. The exact options are: (a) client-authoritative ground (client has GPU; changes netcode trust), or (b) give the server a headless-GL context to bake the same field. WITNESS the limit correctly: compare GPU probe(d) vs CPU fn(d) at the SAME input d (isolates pure float, no mapping/coordinate confound) - stabilize the probe per-d. A faceWarp/coordinate-mapping hypothesis is a common RED HERRING when the real residual is float - test same-d first.